---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Stitch from "../components/ui/Stitch.astro";
import ProjectCard from "../components/ProjectCard.astro";
---

<Page
	title="About — Automate Ethically"
	description="Who's behind this, what we're building, and what we intend to become."
	ogImage="/og/about.png"
>
	<section class="ae-hero" aria-labelledby="about-title">
		<p class="ae-eyebrow">About</p>
		<h1 id="about-title">Neighbors, builders, and organizers.</h1>
		<p class="ae-hero-sub">
			Not a campaign, not yet a committee — here's exactly what this is
			today, and what we build alongside it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<Stitch />

	<section class="ae-section" id="projects" aria-labelledby="projects-title">
		<div class="ae-container">
			<p class="ae-eyebrow">Built on these principles</p>
			<h2 class="ae-h2" id="projects-title">Proof, in working code</h2>
			<div class="ae-projects">
				<!-- DRAFT: rewrite — copy task 6: project card jacquard -->
				<ProjectCard name="jacquard" chip="in development" chipQuiet>
					<p>
						Version control where a decision is an object in the graph. An
						agent can draft a decision and propose it.
					</p>
					<p class="ae-project-human">
						Only a person can settle it — with a few sentences in their own
						words, under their own name.
					</p>
				</ProjectCard>
				<!-- DRAFT: rewrite — copy task 7: project card phantom-thread -->
				<ProjectCard name="phantom-thread" chip="request an invite">
					<p>
						A careful review and a rubber stamp leave the same green check.
						phantom-thread makes them distinguishable — and rewards the
						careful one.
					</p>
					<p>
						<a href="https://jdetle.com/phantom-thread">Preview at
						jdetle.com/phantom-thread</a> (invite-gated today).
					</p>
				</ProjectCard>
			</div>
			<p class="ae-aside">
				The thinking behind these tools lives at
				<a href="https://jdetle.com">jdetle.com</a>.
			</p>

			<!-- DRAFT: rewrite — new copy task: "fellow travelers" list. These
			     are other people's projects, not ours — keep the language clear
			     that this is a pointer list, not an endorsement we've verified
			     beyond reading the project itself. -->
			<div class="ae-prose">
				<h3 class="ae-h2">Other people building the same way</h3>
				<p>
					We didn't invent "accountability enforced by the build" — a few
					other open source projects already treat it as an engineering
					requirement, not a policy ask:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<a href="https://github.com/dssg/aequitas">Aequitas</a> (University
						of Chicago) — audits a classifier for biased outcomes before it's
						used on bail, hiring, or benefits decisions.
					</li>
					<li>
						<a href="https://github.com/Trusted-AI/AIF360">AI Fairness 360</a>
						(IBM) and <a href="https://github.com/fairlearn/fairlearn">Fairlearn</a>
						(Microsoft) — general-purpose toolkits for detecting and mitigating
						biased models.
					</li>
					<li>
						<a href="https://github.com/pymetrics/audit-ai">audit-AI</a>
						(pymetrics) — built specifically to audit hiring and assessment
						algorithms for discriminatory patterns.
					</li>
					<li>
						<a href="https://github.com/open-policy-agent/opa">Open Policy
						Agent</a> — a CNCF-graduated policy engine that turns "a decision
						needs sign-off" into enforceable code instead of a handbook line.
					</li>
					<li>
						<a href="https://github.com/activepieces/activepieces">Activepieces</a>
						— self-hosted workflow automation with human-approval steps built
						into the flow graph itself, not bolted on after.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="ae-aside">
					We haven't used any of these ourselves — they're listed because
					they share the principle, not because we've audited the code. If
					you maintain one of these and something here is wrong, tell us:
					<a href="mailto:join@automate-ethically.com?subject=Build%20page%20correction">join@automate-ethically.com</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<Stitch />

	<!-- DRAFT: rewrite — copy task 8: movement / PAC-goal statement. This
	     sentence relocates here verbatim from the index page — do not
	     rewrite it without re-checking README's "PAC is a goal, not an
	     entity" content rule. -->
	<section class="ae-section ae-section--tint" id="movement" aria-labelledby="movement-title">
		<div class="ae-container">
			<p class="ae-eyebrow">The movement</p>
			<h2 class="ae-h2" id="movement-title">Don't hate the player. Change the rulebook.</h2>
			<p class="ae-plain">
				We intend to form a registered political committee. Until that
				exists, we are neighbors, builders, and organizers — and we are not
				asking for money.
			</p>
			<p class="ae-aside">
				Curious what "not asking for money" actually means in practice? See
				<a href="/toolkit/faq#whos-behind-this-are-you-raising-money">the FAQ</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<Stitch />

	<!-- DRAFT: rewrite — copy task 22: who-is-John block -->
	<section class="ae-section" aria-labelledby="who-title">
		<div class="ae-container">
			<p class="ae-eyebrow">Who's behind this</p>
			<h2 class="ae-h2" id="who-title">One person, so far.</h2>
			<p class="ae-prose">
				This site, the toolkit, and the two projects above are built by
				<a href="https://jdetle.com">John Detlefs</a>. If you're reading
				this before the movement has a staff or a committee, that's because
				it doesn't have one yet — see <a href="/act">how to help build
				one</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Page>
